1.5.8.m:
"Apportion, om!" the sacrificer consents.
Then he apportions [it]
three times with this formula: "At the impulse of the divine Savitar, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, I apportion you, agreeable to Agni!";

2.5.39.m:
"In the lion be my wrath;
in the tiger be my internal malady;
in the wolf be my hunger;
in the horse be my voracity;

in the desert be my thirst;
in the royal house be my craving for food;
in the stone be my sluggishness;

in the donkey be my hemorrhoids;
in the porcupine be my shame;
in the aśvattha tree be my trembling;
in the turtle be my bodily affliction;

in the he-goat be my wandering off;
in the enemy be my death;
in the rival be my evil;

in the foe be my ruin;
in ill repute be my failure;
in the wild ass be my lack of success;

in the rhinoceros be my distress;
in the gavaya be my blindness;
in the wild ox be my deafness;
in the bear be my grief;

in the monitor lizard be my weariness;
in old age be my chill;
in the black bird be my timidity;

in the weasel be my foul odor;
in the owl be my terror of wolves;
in the screech-owl be my jealousy;

in the camel be my thirst;
in the white-footed antelope be my fatigue;
in the ewe be my sheep-disease;
in the receptacle be my odor;

in the maiden be my ornamentation;
in the pig be my mucus;
in the viper be my sleep;

2.5.40.m:
in the python be my nightmare;
in lightning be my mocking laughter;
in longing be my wetness;

in the locust be my evil and misfortune;
in women be my falsehood;
in goats be my rough speech;

in the outcaste be my misfortune;
in the śūdra be my theft;
in the vaiśya be my neglect of duty;

in the unkingly noble be my ignorance;
in the hunter be my killing of a brāhmaṇa;
in the sparrow be my sneezing;

in the ulala bird be my wantonness;
in the otter be my vomiting;
in the kimpuruṣa be my weeping;

in the leopard be my burning pain;
in the elephant be my leprosy;
in the dog be my defilement;

among the Snāvanyas be my barbarian speech;
among the Videhas be my swollen feet;
among the Mahāvarṣas be my goiter;

among the Mūjavants be my burning fever;
in the drum be my cough;
among the Ikṣvākus be my bile;

among the Kaliṅgas be my impurity;
in the mule be my childlessness;
in the harlot be my misconduct;

in the parrot be my jaundice;
in the peacock be my prattle;
in the bull be my decrepitude;
in the blue jay be my calumny;

in the waters be my fatigue;
in the abandoner of sacred study be my offense.
Go away, Evil, be destroyed again!
Place us, O Evil, in the world of the well-done, O Evil, undisturbed!
Whoever does not abandon us, O Evil,
him indeed we abandon;
let the thousand-eyed, immortal one
settle elsewhere than with us!
Whoever hates us, let him come to harm;
and whom we hate, him do you slay!"

2.6.41.m:
In the very same way that of the āhavanīya fire;
in the very same way those of the other two fires if he is going to establish them.
Then, having gone out northwards along the landmark between the altar and the rubbish heap, they go in that direction where his ritual requisites have been prepared.

2.9.48.m:
He wins that.
Twelve, the eldest;
he wins that.
Twelve, the younger [brother];

This same rule applies to three;
this same applies to two.
Saying 'It is made, it is made!' they depart.
